{"name":"Champagne Toast","hex":"#f4e1c6","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"champagne","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A sparkling white wine made from a blend of grapes, especially Chardonnay and pinot, produced in Champagne, France, by the méthode champenoise.","Any sparkling wine made by the méthode champenoise."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a very pale brownish-gold colour, similar to that of champagne.","Alternative form of champaine."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/champagne"},{"term":"toast","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Bread that has been toasted (cooked lightly by browning).","A proposed salutation (e.g. saying \"cheers\") while drinking alcohol."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cook lightly by browning by means of direct exposure to a fire or other heat source.","To cook lightly by browning under a grill or in a toaster; to grill."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/toast"}],"entries":[{"title":"Toast (honor)","blocks":[{"text":"A toast is a ritual during which a drink is taken as an expression of honour or goodwill. The term may be applied to the person, animal or object so honoured, the drink taken, or the verbal expression accompanying the drink. Thus, a person could be \"the toast of the evening\", for whom someone \"proposes a toast\" to congratulate and for whom a third person \"toasts\" in agreement. The ritual forms the basis of the literary and performance genre, of which Mark Twain's \"To the Babies\" is a well-known example."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Barack_Obama_and_Julia_Gillard_toast_Nov_16_2011_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Barack_Obama_and_Julia_Gillard_toast_Nov_16_2011_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Toast_(honor)","wikidata":"Q154168"},{"title":"Champagne","blocks":[{"text":"Champagne is a sparkling wine originated and produced in the Champagne wine region of France under the AOC rules of the appellation, which demand specific vineyard practices, sourcing of grapes exclusively from designated places within it, specific grape-pressing methods and secondary fermentation of the wine in the bottle to cause carbonation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Glass_of_champagne.jpg/330px-Glass_of_champagne.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Champagne","wikidata":"Q134862"},{"title":"Champagne (color)","blocks":[{"text":"The color champagne is a name given for various very pale tints of yellowish-orange that are close to beige. The color's name is derived from the typical color of the beverage Champagne."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3e/Quartz-Topaz-gem7-76d.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Champagne_(color)","wikidata":"Q5069879"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Hip%2C_Hip%2C_Hurrah%21_Artists%E2%80%99_Party%2C_Skagen_%28Peder_Severin_Kr%C3%B8yer%29_-_Gothenburg_Museum_of_Art_-_F_62.tif/lossy-page1-500px-Hip%2C_Hip%2C_Hurrah%21_Artists%E2%80%99_Party%2C_Skagen_%28Peder_Severin_Kr%C3%B8yer%29_-_Gothenburg_Museum_of_Art_-_F_62.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Hip%2C_Hip%2C_Hurrah%21_Artists%E2%80%99_Party%2C_Skagen_%28Peder_Severin_Kr%C3%B8yer%29_-_Gothenburg_Museum_of_Art_-_F_62.tif/lossy-page1-1280px-Hip%2C_Hip%2C_Hurrah%21_Artists%E2%80%99_Party%2C_Skagen_%28Peder_Severin_Kr%C3%B8yer%29_-_Gothenburg_Museum_of_Art_-_F_62.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Hip%2C_Hip%2C_Hurrah%21_Artists%E2%80%99_Par